  <Fragment slot="lede">Hire them the way you'd hire anyone senior: decide what the job is, ask every candidate the same questions, and judge what they can do, not how much you like them.</Fragment>

  <h2>The short answer</h2>

  <p>Write down what you need them to own before you meet anyone. Then put every candidate through the same questions, scored against that list. Ask each one to walk you through a decision they made and why, including one they got wrong. Ask how your team will run on the days they aren't there, and what they'll leave behind when they go. Check references you can actually reach.</p>

  <h2>Decide what the job is first</h2>

  <p>Most bad hires start before the first interview, with a role nobody defined. Fractional is no different. Before you talk to anyone, write a short list: the outcomes this person will own, the decisions they can make without you, the team they'll lead, and how you'll know in three months that it's working.</p>

  <p>That list becomes your scorecard. Without it, you end up choosing the person who interviewed best, which isn't the same thing.</p>

  <h2>Ask everyone the same questions</h2>

  <p><a href="https://hbr.org/2016/04/how-to-take-the-bias-out-of-interviews" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Unstructured interviews are a poor guide</a> to how someone will actually do the job. Structured ones, where every candidate gets the same questions and the same criteria, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8325.1988.tb00467.x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">predict performance roughly twice as well</a>. They also feel fairer to the people you're interviewing.</p>

  <p>Questions worth asking every candidate:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>What would you want to own here, and what would you leave with me?</li>
    <li>Talk me through a product decision you made. What were the options, and why that one?</li>
    <li>Tell me about a call you got wrong. When did you notice, and what did you do?</li>
    <li>How will my team keep moving on the days you're not here?</li>
    <li>When you leave, what will we have that we don't have now?</li>
    <li>Who could I speak to about working with you?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Signs it will work</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>They ask about your goals and your team before they talk about themselves.</li>
    <li>They can explain the reasons behind their past decisions, not just the results.</li>
    <li>They talk about owning outcomes, not producing reports.</li>
    <li>They describe how they'll build up your people, not how they'll replace them.</li>
    <li>They're clear about what they won't do.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Signs it won't</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Every answer is a framework, and none of them fits your situation.</li>
    <li>They can't name a decision they got wrong.</li>
    <li>The plan depends on them being there indefinitely.</li>
    <li>You like them a lot, and can't say what they'd change.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>That last one deserves a second look. Likeable and capable are different questions. Ask whether they can do the job, and what support they'd need to do it well.</p>

  <h2>Check the references</h2>

  <p>Ask for people you can look up and contact yourself, with names and roles. Ask the same few questions of each: what did this person own, what changed while they were there, and what did the team look like after they left? If a reference can only say they were great to work with, keep asking.</p>

  <h2>What a good handover looks like</h2>

  <p>A fractional engagement should end with less dependence on the leader, not more. By the last month, look for a team that runs without them, a way of working someone else can pick up, and decisions that stay decided. If a candidate can describe that end state on day one, that's a good sign.</p>

  <h2>When to talk to me</h2>

  <p>If you're weighing up a fractional product leader and want to test the role before you commit, <a href="/fractional/">a short call</a> can help you write the scorecard. You'll leave with clearer questions, whoever you hire.</p>

